WEBVTT

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.100
At the beginning of this century, two last glittering prizes remained to be won,

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.140
the North and the South Poles.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:53.250
The explorers of three nations competed to be the first at the ends of the earth,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.520
Norway, the United States, and Great Britain.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.000
Then suddenly, and unexpectedly, in 1936,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.860
1909, an American on a dog sledge got to the North Pole first. Only one goal now remained,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.570
the South Pole. In Britain, popular attention had been centred on the South Pole for some

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:20.980
time. In 1902, Captain Scott had got to within 430 miles of it. Six years later, an Irishman,

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:30.000
Shackleton, turned back only 100 miles short of it. So, by 1910, when Captain Scott,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.700
had raised the backing to try again, the entire nation was in a high state of

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:40.690
excited expectation. Apparently he had no rival. He was bound to win. What nobody

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:45.260
in Britain knew was that there was in fact a most serious and secretive

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:51.200
competitor about to show his hand. A Norwegian called Roald Amundsen. Amundsen

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.230
had been bitterly disappointed by the American achievement in the North. He had

00:01:55.230 --> 00:02:00.000
been planning to drift across the North Pole in the pack ice. And indeed as Captain

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.890
Scott now headed south in his ship the Terra Nova, everyone thought that Amundsen was still

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.050
intent upon crossing the North Pole that way. But when Amundsen aboard his ship the Fram

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:15.730
got to Madeira, he sent a telegram to Captain Scott, who by this time had reached Melbourne.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:23.390
It said simply, "Beg leave to inform you, preceding Antarctica." The secret was out.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.270
Amundsen had changed his plans.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:30.000
Because of the American success in the North, Amundsen's backers had withdrawn their final

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.860
financial support. There was only one thing he could do. He was going to try to restore

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.250
his fortunes and his prestige by beating Scott to the South Pole.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.700
Ice Barrier, Antarctica. January 1911, 788 miles from the South Pole. Amundsen and his

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:16.650
men had landed from the Fram, one day ahead of schedule after a voyage of 16,000 miles.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:20.520
400 miles away, Scott had already established his base.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.680
Captain Raul Amundsen was 38.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.560
As a boy, he had been enthralled by the exploits of polar explorers.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.610
What appealed to me was the suffering they endured.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.000
A strange ambition burned within me to experience those

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:07.650
same sufferings. At 22, Amundsen abandoned his career as a doctor. He spent a winter

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.960
in the Antarctic, and in the Arctic, on his Northwest Passage expedition, he learned the

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.300
techniques of dog sledging from the Eskimos. Now he faced his greatest challenge. His own

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:29.830
reputation and the honor of Norway were at stake. Our base camp, which we called Frumheim,

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.280
was 60 miles nearer the pole than Scots. Unlike Scots party, all my men were used to working

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:44.790
in cold climates. Jürgen Stuberer was the youngest, a carpenter from Oslo. Then there

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:52.050
was Olaf Björlund, a national ski champion. In charge of stores, Oskar Vistin from Larvik,

00:04:52.050 --> 00:05:00.000
a trained navigator. Captain Gelmar Johansson at 40.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.060
before the oldest and the most experienced.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.790
He'd been two years with Nansen on his North Polar journey.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:12.570
Lieutenant Prestru from the Navy, responsible for scientific observations.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:20.000
Sverre Hassel was from Oslo.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.510
He'd spent four years in the Arctic.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:30.000
Helmar Hansen had been with me on the Northwest Passage expedition.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:40.770
could drive dogs like an Eskimo. 97 Greenland Huskies had been taken aboard in Norway, and

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:47.870
19 puppies were born on the voyage. The dogs were a little unruly at first. After six months

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:55.650
at sea, work came as an unpleasant surprise. I knew that Scott planned to start with ponies,

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:00.000
dogs, and even tractors, and then manhole his sledges.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.620
for the final assault on the pole.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.800
But it was obvious to me that dogs were the more suitable transport.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.630
If a dog falls down a crevasse, no great harm is done.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.990
A tug at his harness and he is out again.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:16.950
But it is another matter that a pony or a motor sledge...

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:22.270
Altogether, the dogs hold 900 boxes of stores from the from to our base camp.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.740
The final sledge journey brought in the ninth member of our expedition,

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:28.600
Adolf Lindström, the cook.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.000
Aha, Lindström!

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.970
Now it's going to be really good food to get after that, my lord.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:41.730
On January the 28th, 1911, we moved into our new home, Framman.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:53.570
Amundsen's relationship with his men was based on mutual trust.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:57.010
He alone had picked them, and they respected him.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.180
To them all, Amundsen was the boss.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.820
But unlike Captain Scott, he shared the same living quarters

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:14.800
in the hut that served as dormitory, dining room, conference room, and kitchen combined.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.490
Yet at the same time, Amundsen was not a man to be contradicted.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:29.960
What he said...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:41.590
We now had two months of daylight left before the sun began to disappear, leaving the perpetual

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:50.220
night of the Antarctic winter. My plan was to use those two months of valuable daylight

00:07:50.220 --> 00:08:00.000
to set up food depots on part of the 788 mile journey to the South Pole. These depots

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.040
would be 60 miles apart and would save carrying so much food on the polar

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:15.320
journey. We set out to lay the first of the depots on February the 10th. It was a

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:21.410
time for testing our techniques. As well as sledge-mounted compasses, we relied on

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:27.780
two other methods for accurate navigation: meter wheels, which recorded

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.570
the distances covered each day.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:37.300
and sightings of the sun through a sextant to fix accurately our position in the featureless landscape.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:48.390
At each depot, we built a beacon of snow blocks with a flag on top so that we could spot it in bad weather.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.420
And on either side of the depot, we set up more flags.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.770
we had laid out three tons of supplies before the winter came

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:11.040
it was not all plain sailing bad weather prevented us from establishing a depot

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:30.000
at 83 degrees south and eight dogs died through cold and exhaustion each of our

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.160
huskies needs two pounds of meat a day. On the polar journey, I plan to supplement this

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:42.380
by killing the weakest animals and feeding them to the strongest. There is about 50 pounds

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:47.950
of edible flesh on an Eskimo dog, so every dog slaughtered would mean 50 pounds less

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:59.160
dog food to be carried. On April the 19th,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.610
our last sight of the sun for six months, though some daylight still remains.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:11:00.000
The snow drifts caused some frowns in the morning.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.120
one had a good idea.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.620
Instead of clearing the snow,

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.120
why not excavate

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.330
and hollow out a carpenter shop

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:08.370
in the biggest rift?

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:11.950
I'm going to Biodan

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:13.010
to see how it goes with sleene.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:14.620
What will you, Captain?

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:29.440
One excavation had led to another.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.240
And soon, we had a whole underground village.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.470
We now had the space to perfect our equipment for the polar journey.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:47.190
Tents and clothing were altered by this thing.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:52.760
Of hint!

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:56.790
The sledges had proved much too heavy for the dogs to pull easily.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:08.710
It was Stubere and Björlund who had the job of shaving them down from 165 pounds to 48.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:17.350
After hard working days, there was time to relax in the warmth and comfort of the hut.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.230
Time, too, to explain what lay ahead.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.310
plan was different from Scott's. I intended to use a new route to the pole and drive straight

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:44.760
through, regardless of obstacles. We'd leave the food depots for the return journey. We

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:49.630
would slaughter some dogs as we approached the pole. This would give the men and surviving

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.010
dogs fresh meat on the way home.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:08.130
Johansen raised the question of the mountains. Shackleton had discovered three years before

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:14.310
that the polar plateau was 10,000 feet above sea level. So, of course, we knew there must

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:20.200
be mountains between us and the pole. I said, we will go straight through.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:30.000
We made a maternity igloo for the husky bitches to prevent

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.670
their puppies being eaten by the other dogs a fate that had overtaken earlier

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:52.210
litters the weather observations were now in full swing and readings were made at

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:58.950
8 a.m. 2 in the afternoon and 8 o'clock at night the outside temperature was

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:00.000
often below minus

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.680
58 degrees Fahrenheit maximum minus 4.50,4 minimum minus 1.60,2

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:24.150
How much weight do we have? 24 kg

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:30.000
Tubera and Bjørland had made a good job of the sledges. They had got away

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.570
down to 53 pounds, only 5 pounds short of what I wanted.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:37.460
You'd make it, you'd make it.

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:43.300
June the 23rd. Midwinter in the Antarctic, but Midsummer's Eve in Norway.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:46.160
Traditionally, the time for celebration.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.040
Cholabim!

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.200
Lindstrom had done us proud.

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.120
His Napoleon cakes were served

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:17.050
after each man had put away

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:18.250
a seal meat stew

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:20.280
and a quart of a plum pudding.

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:22.700
They made our mouths water.

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:24.910
The finest puff pastry

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.930
with layers of vanilla custard

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:28.130
and cream.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:55.920
It's a song called Solveig Song by Grieg, a favorite with all Norwegians, reminding them of home and their loved ones.

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:24.830
For Amundsen, "home" meant creditors and explanations about his sudden change in plan.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:28.710
Unless, of course, he could claim the South Pole for Norway.

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:40.970
They all sensed that Amundsen was nervous,

00:16:40.970 --> 00:16:56.160
that he was worrying that Scott would get there first.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:00.000
It was essential that we were fit and ready to go at the earliest

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:22.740
Possible moment. Sledge rations were packed and the sledges loaded underground and then brought up by tackle through a hole in the clothing store.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.570
Now it's been a little to wait.

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:34.680
I don't know.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:39.780
By August 27, we were ready to go.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:43.240
But the weather was still too cold.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:45.040
Amundsen fretted at the delay.

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:47.140
He kept thinking about Scott.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:51.510
Johansen warned him that even if it got warmer at this time of year,

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.580
it was likely to be only temporary.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.960
Ah, let's see what I mean.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:59.030
Last night it was -9:40.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.550
Minus seven. A rise of 18 degrees. It was enough for Amundsen.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:13.700
On September the 8th, the eight-man party set out for the poll, against Johansson's advice.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:18.730
Lindström was left with his doubts too. It was a Friday, and he was superstitious.

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:41.180
"September the 11th, Johansson's Diary traveled 15.2 miles, minus 56 Fahrenheit.

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:50.170
The breath of men and dogs freezes the moment it hits the air."

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:57.270
On the same day, Amundsen wrote, "Mercifully, there is no wind, so that nobody feels the

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:10.070
The 12th. Only four miles today. Minus 63.4. All the compasses frozen.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:13.930
We therefore stopped and built two igloos for shelter.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:23.710
The outlook for milder weather is doubtful.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:29.860
I have no choice but to dump our supplies when we reach the 80-degree depot.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:36.900
and return to Framheim to wait for warmer weather.

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:48.490
The 13th, the severe cold saps the dog's capacity to pull the sledges.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:50.710
Many have blood on their feet.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:53.430
We should never have started so soon.

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.440
There is only one explanation.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:19:58.870
Scott.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:06.640
On the 14th, they reached the 80-degree depot, unloaded their stores, and turned for home.

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.210
The cold was still intense, well below minus 60.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:26.420
Hermanson, with two sledges and five of the men, made good headway.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:29.180
But things didn't go so well for Johansson.

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:38.250
The 16th, Trestru and I were soon left far behind.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:44.790
After 12 miles, we began to wonder if we could ever catch up with others.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:21:00.000
The journey was taking its toll of the dogs, one of Stubroos had dropped dead in its tracks.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.880
Reached from him at midnight.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.480
We had had neither drink nor anything warm to eat since five in the morning.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.980
Prestrud's feet were badly frostbitten.

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:17.390
How do you reach the foot?

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:18.870
Damn.

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:27.550
This journey was to create what Johansson called a sad drop of poison.

00:21:28.690 --> 00:21:30.000
This is going to make it worse.

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:38.390
The next morning the others were already at breakfast when Johansen got up

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:56.580
by the exertions of their abortive journey.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:59.890
But clearly there had to be a post-mortem into what had gone wrong.

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:03.240
The form it took caught Johansson unprepared.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:12.290
What did you hold up?

00:22:12.290 --> 00:22:17.030
The boss asked me why I had got back so late.

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:18.540
I was astonished.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:21.170
Why did they just ride on?

00:22:21.170 --> 00:22:24.800
I said that his method of traveling was extraordinary.

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:27.330
It wasn't a way to lead an expedition.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.800
It was sheer panic.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:59.190
I was astounded that he saw fit to question both our method of travel and my position as leader was outrageous.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.720
To make matters worse, he had spoken so that all could hear.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:11.060
On a polar journey, the leadership must not be challenged,

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.860
especially by an experienced polar traveller like Johansson,

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.040
for then it is doubly dangerous.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:39.840
The bull must be taken by the horns, and at once.

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:45.920
I announced that in view of Johansson's remarks, he would not be included in the Polar Party.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:54.180
Instead, he would take part in a minor expedition to King Edward VII's land, and to the Commander-Lieutenant Prestru.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:24:00.000
Johansson refused to accept Amundsen's verbal decision, and asked for his orders in writing.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:24.320
He appealed to the others to speak up in his defense, but whatever they thought, they never spoke.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:29.390
Johansen had challenged Amundsons leadership and was now paying the price.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:34.490
They all knew that in a venture like this, there could only be one leader.

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:37.980
It had to be Raoul Amundsen.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:45.030
Johansen never overcame the disgrace of being left behind, unjustly, as he thought.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:48.720
Amundsen had decided to take only five men to the pole.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:53.320
Himself, Hassel, Visting, Hansen, and Bjørland.

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:56.280
Oh, Johansen, it's not too hard.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.680
Yes, so we're going!

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:08.740
They left Framheim on October the 20th, 1911.

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:30.000
The last thing I saw before the known world disappeared

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.160
disappeared, was pressed through, turning the handle of a cinematograph.

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:45.690
October the 21st, really vile weather.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:47.990
We are quite blinded.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:10.920
- I'm going to be quick. - Asil!

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:59.250
The sledge was intact, but the case with our primer stove was hanging by its last thread.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:05.880
Without it, we would have nothing to melt snow for water, cook our food, or dry our clothing.

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:08.180
It was vital to retrieve it.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.200
Come in! Come in!

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:15.940
Good job, I think.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:18.430
And then, up again.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:21.300
Then we take the sled.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:23.180
Come in!

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:24.480
Be careful!

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:24.800
Be careful!

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:35.620
We didn't waste much time in leaving that place.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:51.920
For the next 21 days, we kept up an average of 20 miles a day.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:59.030
We were now beyond our depots, nearly halfway to the port, and on unknown ground.

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:28.870
After the glare of the snowfields, the interior of the five-man tent was restful to the eyes.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:51.960
With the unloading finished, we had to protect the canvas of the tent from the attention of the dogs.

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:56.660
So we provided them with a snow wall around the base of the tent.

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:07.330
November the 12th, position 84 degrees south.

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:18.400
In the far distance, we saw a chain of mountains running across our front.

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:29.120
We had now reached one of the most critical points of our journey.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:36.130
the top of the mountains lay the polar plateau. When we got there, with the going easier, we

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:47.550
would slaughter 24 of our dogs, leaving 18 to continue the journey. We began to climb on November

00:29:47.550 --> 00:30:00.000
the 17th. The sun was so warm, we even had to take off some of our clothes. On some of the steeper slopes,

00:30:00.510 --> 00:30:02.690
we had to use two dog teams to a sledge.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:06.360
And even then, we had to give them a helping hand ourselves.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:16.360
But the dogs exceeded all our expectations.

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:20.210
The first day, we climbed 2,000 feet.

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:22.640
The next day, 3,000.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:25.580
On the third day, 750.

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:29.900
And on the fourth, more than 5,000 feet.

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:35.480
At the top, we found we had not yet reached a level polar plateau.

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:38.000
There were still high ridges ahead of us.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:41.740
But we had climbed over 11,000 feet in four days.

00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:48.020
We are gred. We are up.

00:30:48.550 --> 00:30:50.410
Here from the strong road.

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:10.630
We were over the mountains proper six days ahead of schedule.

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:13.890
And on November the 21st, we made camp.

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:24.070
We called this place the Butchers.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:29.090
24 of our brave and faithful companions were marked out for death.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.900
Each man was responsible for shooting his own dogs.

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:39.800
I hoped the pumping of the Primus would cut out the sound of the shots.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.210
At first, the dogs didn't like the entrails.

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:59.450
That night, we treated ourselves to dog cutlets.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.000
It smells good.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:05.410
I hope it smells like good.

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:09.350
Yes, I'll try it on the dance.

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:11.400
Yes, I'll try it on him.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:19.210
It's good.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:21.030
I'm good.

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:22.410
It was excellent.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:23.910
Absolutely excellent.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.220
following day, the weather changed for the worst, and we were pinned down at the butchers.

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:48.360
For four days, the blizzard blew. In their tent, the Norwegians slept. They had divided the

00:32:48.360 --> 00:32:54.180
surviving dogs into three teams and repacked the sledges. All of them were feeling the effect of

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:54.820
the high altitude.

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:22.310
On the fifth day, impatient at the delay, we decided to press on despite the weather.

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.450
The dogs were not much inclined to start.

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:37.460
Our tent was so covered in ice we had to handle it carefully or it could have been badly damaged.

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:12.020
We were running blind over unknown ground.

00:34:12.950 --> 00:34:13.750
It was sheer madness.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:17.680
I was afraid we might fall into a chasm before we could pull up.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:57.180
I gave the order to put up the tent and wait for better weather.

00:34:57.180 --> 00:34:59.730
As it turned out, we had stopped just in time.

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:25.960
On the very edge of an abyss, tree in the morning, the sun broke through the mist.

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:29.760
It gave us a chance to get our bearings and work out a new route.

00:35:41.360 --> 00:35:43.760
Do you want to come down there?

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:47.200
Yes, it's good.

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:50.620
I think we'll get some more sleep before we move forward.

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:56.760
But the weather is soon closed in again.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:16.960
For the next 11 days, they stumbled forward in the kind of conditions which forced Scott,

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:30.000
who was nearly 300 miles behind them, to keep to his tent.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.200
15 miles a day, we picked

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:34.260
our way to an awesome formation of blue

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:35.900
ice, which we called

00:36:35.900 --> 00:36:44.130
the Devil's Glacier. December

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:45.900
the 4th, 1911.

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:48.210
The polar plateau at last.

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:51.500
We could now count with a fair degree of certainty

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.220
on reaching the pole by the

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:56.060
15th of December. Would Scott

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.780
be there already? None of us would

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:00.000
admit we were worried by this.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:05.920
But once Björlund said he could see sledges and men on the horizon.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:13.580
He had only imagined it.

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:37.780
Four days later we passed the farthest point Shackleton had reached.

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:45.920
We were farther south now than any man had been.

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:51.650
December the 12th, only 30 miles to go.

00:37:51.650 --> 00:37:58.480
The way the dogs reacted, you might think that some extraordinary thing was to be found away to the south.

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:02.930
These last few days of sun

00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:05.860
seemed to ripen the sores on our exposed faces.

00:38:06.940 --> 00:38:08.180
The slightest gust of wind

00:38:08.180 --> 00:38:10.360
was like someone cutting you with a blunt knife.

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:46.200
The evening of the 13th was just like Christmas Eve.

00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:52.370
Our flag was taken out and lashed to two ski sticks to be ready when the time came.

00:38:52.370 --> 00:38:56.790
There was a pent-up excitement about what was going to happen in the morning.

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:06.730
It was like being a boy again.

00:39:06.730 --> 00:39:12.980
The next morning was the finest summer weather,

00:39:12.980 --> 00:39:16.130
just as if it had been made for arriving at the end of the Earth.

00:39:16.130 --> 00:39:30.000
For the last time, our sledge meters were reset to zero.

00:39:30.830 --> 00:40:00.000
We advanced that day in much the same mechanical way as before. Not much was said. All our

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:08.700
our concentration was on the flat plane ahead.

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:12.330
We knew that we were only 15 miles from the pole.

00:40:12.330 --> 00:40:15.940
Mile by mile, the sledge meters recorded the narrowing gap.

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:46.910
"We carefully examined the sledge meters. They all showed the full distance, 15 miles. We had reached our goal. There was no sign of Scott. We were the first."

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:59.530
At this time, Scott's party was about 330 miles away to the north, pulling their own sledges.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:12.140
Without a common presence and the same, it would not have been possible, so everyone should be able to plant a flag.

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:19.220
I had decided no one man should plant a flag alone. It had to be done by all of us.

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:29.730
So we will plant you, you dear flag, on the South Pole.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:42.440
I named this white country King Hawke on the 7th's land.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:47.390
It was a simple ceremony. In such places, short ceremonies are best.

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:52.810
5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 12.

00:42:52.810 --> 00:43:00.000
Once we had checked our position,

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:06.080
The final act remained. I assumed that Scott would be the next to find this place,

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:12.050
so I asked him to deliver a note to King Haakon of Norway, giving information of what we had accomplished.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:27.780
The way home was a long one, and so many things might happen to make it impossible for us to give our own account of our expedition.

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:58.420
Other items we left behind for Scott were a sextant and spare mittens.

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:13.290
These, together with the bag containing the letters,

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:17.220
were placed in our spare tent, which could be seen from a great distance.

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:37.790
It was a solemn moment when we bared our heads and said farewell to our polar base and to our flag of Norway.

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:09.920
I am sure some will say that we had good luck.

00:45:10.940 --> 00:45:15.170
But what they call good luck, I call good planning.

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:17.920
So, half step!

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:59.920
Later, on January the 17th, 1912,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.950
Captain Scott's party reached the pole to find the Norwegian flag there.

00:46:04.660 --> 00:46:07.540
Few explorers ever faced a more bitter disappointment.

00:46:08.780 --> 00:46:12.440
Amundsen got back to Framheim uneventfully on the very day he had planned.

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:18.010
His fortunes were restored, but his triumph was overshadowed by the news that Scott and

00:46:18.010 --> 00:46:22.930
his four companions had perished of hunger and exhaustion on their way back from the

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:23.450
South Pole.

00:46:24.420 --> 00:46:29.100
This tragedy, which in some extraordinary way seemed to turn Scott's defeat into a victory,

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:30.000
arose.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.350
strong emotions in Britain. Amundsen, it was said, had stolen a march on Scott in

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:40.530
switching to the South Pole, and his use and treatment of dogs was unsporting, to

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:45.810
say the least. It was unfair, of course. Had Scott used more dogs himself, he might

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:50.590
have made an easy journey to the Pole and back. But there's a certain nobility in

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:55.310
the image of him and his party hauling their own sledges that, at the time,

00:46:55.310 --> 00:47:00.000
produced a deep emotional response. It seems an old controversy in

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:06.580
Now, today, at the South Pole, there is an American scientific station supplied by air,

00:47:06.580 --> 00:47:08.520
and there's a plaque there, too.

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:17.760
On it are engraved the names of five Norwegians and five Englishmen, with two dates a month apart.
